No answer leak detected. Here is the proof.

---

## Problem

Determine the asymptotic distribution of
$$8nB = -8n\ln\!\Big(\sqrt{0.2\,\hat p}+\sqrt{0.8\,(1-\hat p)}\Big),\qquad \hat p=\bar X_n,\; X_i\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p),$$
under $H_0:\,p=0.2$.

## Proof

**Step 1 — Identify the structure.** Define
$$g(p)=\sqrt{0.2\,p}+\sqrt{0.8\,(1-p)},\qquad f(p)=-\ln g(p),\qquad B=f(\hat p).$$
Note $g(p)$ is the Bhattacharyya coefficient between $\mathrm{Bernoulli}(0.2)$ and $\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p)$, and $f(p)=-\ln g(p)$ is the (half) Hellinger-type divergence. Under $H_0$, $\hat p\xrightarrow{a.s.}0.2$ by the LLN, and
$$g(0.2)=\sqrt{0.04}+\sqrt{0.64}=0.2+0.8=1,\qquad f(0.2)=-\ln 1=0.$$
So $8nB\to 0$ in probability; we need the quadratic term of the Taylor expansion of $f$ around $p_0=0.2$.

**Step 2 — Compute derivatives of $g$ at $p_0=0.2$.**
$$g'(p)=\frac{\sqrt{0.2}}{2\sqrt p}-\frac{\sqrt{0.8}}{2\sqrt{1-p}},\qquad g'(0.2)=\tfrac12-\tfrac12=0.$$
$$g''(p)=-\frac{\sqrt{0.2}}{4\,p^{3/2}}-\frac{\sqrt{0.8}}{4\,(1-p)^{3/2}},$$
$$g''(0.2)=-\frac{1}{4\cdot 0.2}-\frac{1}{4\cdot 0.8}=-\frac{1}{0.8}-\frac{1}{3.2}=-1.25-0.3125=-\frac{25}{16}.$$

**Step 3 — Derivatives of $f=-\ln g$ at $p_0=0.2$.** Since $f'=-g'/g$ and $f''=-(g''g-(g')^2)/g^2$, using $g(0.2)=1$, $g'(0.2)=0$:
$$f'(0.2)=0,\qquad f''(0.2)=-g''(0.2)=\frac{25}{16}.$$

**Step 4 — Quadratic approximation of $B$.** By Taylor expansion (the remainder is $o((\hat p-p_0)^2)=o_p(1/n)$ since $\hat p-p_0=O_p(n^{-1/2})$):
$$B=f(\hat p)=\tfrac12 f''(0.2)(\hat p-0.2)^2+o_p(1/n)=\frac{25}{32}(\hat p-0.2)^2+o_p(1/n).$$
Hence
$$8nB=\frac{25}{4}\,n(\hat p-0.2)^2+o_p(1).$$

**Step 5 — CLT for $\hat p$.** Under $H_0$, $\mathrm{Var}(X_i)=0.2\cdot 0.8=0.16=\frac{4}{25}$, so by the classical CLT
$$\sqrt n\,(\hat p-0.2)\xrightarrow{d}N\!\Big(0,\frac{4}{25}\Big).$$
Writing $\sqrt n(\hat p-0.2)\xrightarrow{d}\frac{2}{5}Z$ with $Z\sim N(0,1)$, we get
$$n(\hat p-0.2)^2\xrightarrow{d}\frac{4}{25}\,Z^2=\frac{4}{25}\,\chi^2_1.$$

**Step 6 — Combine.** By Slutsky's theorem,
$$8nB\xrightarrow{d}\frac{25}{4}\cdot\frac{4}{25}\,\chi^2_1=\chi^2_1.$$

## Conclusion

Under $H_0:p=0.2$,
$$\boxed{\,8nB\;\xrightarrow{d}\;\chi^2_1\,}.$$

This is the expected general result: $8n$ times the log-Bhattacharyya divergence between the empirical distribution and the null equals, to second order, the Wald statistic $n(\hat p-p_0)^2/\mathrm{Var}(X_i)$, whose limit is $\chi^2_1$ with one degree of freedom (one parameter estimated, one constraint under $H_0$).

### PROOF COMPLETE
